Algorithmic Bias: Literature Review on Gender Biased Algorithms of Social Media Platforms
Shengkai WANG, City University of Hong Kong

Introduction
Adding just two words (like "for women" or "for students") can bring a significant difference in advertisment impressions compared with neutral advertisements (Cecere et al., 2024). These phenomena are examples of algorithmic bias. Algorithmic bias on social media platforms is a systematic skew in the content pushed to users based on their previous behaviour on the platform. This can also be affected by how the algorithms are designed (Baer, 2019). It stems from historical prejudices and the content used to design the algorithms (Mikołajczyk-Bareła & Grochowski, 2023). It is not like educational biases or healthcare biases since social media platforms serve billions of people all over the world. A small piece of content may influence millions of people daily. Although the companies behind the social media platforms say that personalised content can enhance the users' experience when they are using the platforms, they should still prove that their algorithms should not be gender-biased in order not to lead to serious gender problems or social norms. The roadmap of this literature review is firstly examining the harms of gender-biased algorithms, then talking about the counterargument, which is regarded as free speech and marketing events.

Systematic Exclusion
Social media platforms do not always reflect user preferences; they limit the exposure opportunities to certain users based on their existing gender-biased algorithms. Cecere et al.(2024)'s field tests show that adding gender-specific keywords can lead to different advertisement deliveries. This is not a bug. This is a feature of unregulated learning. This kind of situation will lead to prejudice. The algorithm is based on past data where women were already excluded, and now it's repeating the cycle. This limits young users of social media platforms from suitable work opportunities even before they enter the workforce.

Why Self-Regulation Fails
The "black-box" nature of machine learning makes correction of bias impossible and leaves victims without recourse. As de Souza Santos et al.(2025) said in their work: "Inaccuracies within datasets used to train algorithms result in discriminatory outcomes in software applications and reinforce inequalities." The deep learning models are "mathematically hidden", and this leads to impossible for users to appeal legally (de Souza Santos et al., 2025). If users cannot know the reason why they are denied by particular kinds of advertisements, they cannot challenge it. Companies always say that algorithmic bias is a "Technology Limitation", as they don't want to change the biased algorithms. Without external eyes, the bias will remain undetected, and it will operate silently in the background.

